(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["mth-12-04"] = {"4.1":{"s":"Two straight lines given by a₁x + b₁y + c₁ = 0 and a₂x + b₂y + c₂ = 0 together make a pair of lines. Their combined equation is the product of the two expressions set equal to zero. Multiplying out gives a second degree equation in x and y. For lines through the origin the product has no linear or constant terms.","f":[{"x":"(a_1*x + b_1*y)*(a_2*x + b_2*y) = 0","n":"Combined equation of two lines through the origin; expanding gives a*x^2 + 2*h*x*y + b*y^2 = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.114"}],"w":["The product of two line equations, set to zero, represents both lines together."]},"4.2":{"s":"A homogeneous equation of degree two in x and y has the form ax² + 2hxy + by² = 0: every term has degree two. The combined equation of a pair of lines through the origin is always of this type. The reverse holds when h² − ab ≥ 0: then the equation represents two real lines through the origin. Substituting y = mx gives an auxiliary equation for the slopes.","f":[{"x":"a*x^2 + 2*h*x*y + b*y^2 = 0","n":"Homogeneous equation of degree two; represents a pair of lines through the origin if h^2 - a*b >= 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.114"},{"x":"b*m^2 + 2*h*m + a = 0","n":"Auxiliary equation: its roots m_1, m_2 are the slopes of the two lines.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.118"},{"x":"m_1 + m_2 = -2*h/b","n":"Sum of the slopes; the product is m_1*m_2 = a/b.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.118"}],"w":["x² + y² = 0 is homogeneous of degree two but is not a pair of real lines: h² − ab = −1 < 0."]},"4.3":{"s":"The acute angle θ between the pair of lines ax² + 2hxy + by² = 0 depends only on a, h and b. The lines are perpendicular when the coefficients of x² and y² add to zero (a + b = 0) and coincident when h² = ab. Lines at a given angle to a given line come from the slope formula for that angle.","f":[{"x":"tan(theta) = |2*sqrt(h^2 - a*b)/(a + b)|","n":"Acute angle between the lines ax^2 + 2hxy + by^2 = 0.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.123"},{"x":"a + b = 0","n":"Condition for the lines to be perpendicular.","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.125"},{"x":"h^2 = a*b","n":"Condition for the lines to coincide (equal slopes).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.115"}],"w":["If a + b = 0 the tangent formula has zero below: the angle is a right angle, not undefined."]},"4.4":{"s":"The general second degree equation ax² + 2hxy + by² + 2gx + 2fy + c = 0 represents a pair of straight lines only if abc + 2fgh − af² − bg² − ch² = 0 (the determinant of the coefficients is zero) and h² − ab ≥ 0. When it does, the lines are perpendicular if a + b = 0, parallel if h² = ab; they meet at a point given by the coefficients.","f":[{"x":"a*b*c + 2*f*g*h - a*f^2 - b*g^2 - c*h^2 = 0","n":"Condition for the general second degree equation to represent a pair of lines (together with h^2 - a*b >= 0).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.125"},{"x":"((h*f - b*g)/(a*b - h^2), (g*h - a*f)/(a*b - h^2))","n":"Point of intersection of the two lines (when h^2 > a*b).","r":"Balbharati Maths XII Part 1, p.125"}],"w":["Both conditions are needed: the determinant is zero AND h² − ab ≥ 0."]}};
